[Music]  time now for morning rounds our look at  the medical news of the week first up  the benefits of a better diet most of us  are aware there's a relationship between  our food choices and our overall health  but what's the real impact over time  newly published research from the New  England Journal of Medicine examined  data from two large studies they looked  at the relationship between the quality  of one's diet and risk of death by using  three different dietary scoring methods  they found a 20 percentile increase in  diet scores was associated with an 8 to  17 percent reduction in deaths here now  with more on this study a CBS News chief  medical correspondent dr. Jon LaPook and  CBS News contributor dr. Tara Narula  Tara take us through some of the results  from this study so this was a very large  study they looked at about 70,000  participants and they assessed their  diet quality with the scores as you  mentioned over a 12-year period and then  they looked for a 12 year subsequent  period to see what the death rates were  and they found a couple things they  found that those people who had the  poorest scores at baseline and improved  the most at a 15 to 28 percent decrease  in their mortality those people who had  consistently high levels of diet scores  decreased their mortality 9 to 14  percent and on the other hand those  people who had declining diet quality  increased their mortality by about 6 to  12 percent so what is the real takeaway  from this it's a couple things one is  that this is really about healthy  dietary patterns not about one single  nutrient they looked at things like the  Mediterranean diet and the DASH diet the  second is that small changes can have a  big impact so the authors of the paper  discussed this idea that even some  swapping out or substituting out red  meat or processed meat one serving a day  and switching it with legumes and nuts  you can improve your diet quality by  about 20% and the last is that it's  never too late because the majority of  participants were over 60 years old well  that's very interesting John well is  there a difference between say if you  just do this for a couple of years and  if you do it over a longer period of  time yeah as you would expect the answer  is yes so if you kept it up for eight  years mortality went down 11% you kept  it up for 16 years it went down 26% and  if you think we'll what  to study to show us that I cannot tell  you how many patients over the years  when I say you know you need to adjust  your diet they'll say well wait it's  gonna turn out that like in sleeper in  that Woody Allen movie that in the  future it's gonna turn out that deep  fats good for you and it's you know you  may think it's obvious but it's great to  have the data Tara what are some of the  characteristics of the people that  improved their diets over time so those  that improved seem to be younger they  were more physically active they  consumed less alcohol when you looked at  what they were eating no big surprise  they increased the number of whole  grains or the amount of whole grains in  their diet  increase the vegetables the fruits the  omega 3s or the fish that they consumed  and they decrease things like sodium  processed meat or red meat and sugary  beverages it feels like we hear the same  thing and are just constantly reminded  we need to do it our next topic actually  deals with cancer and the social impact  it can have on adolescents and young  adults the study was published in the  journal cancer it examined the social  functioning of this age group within 2  years of their cancer diagnosis there  were 215 participants at the start with  a hundred and forty-one completing the  follow up survey 2020 four months later  all were between the ages of 14 and 39  John this is something we haven't seen a  lot of studies on the social effects of  cancer was one of the big findings here  well as you might expect they tend to  bottom out in terms of their social  function at the time of diagnosis in the  next year they climb up but then they  kind of level off for the next year and  I knew nothing about this until about  five or six years ago I met a guy named  Matthew Zachary who at 21 was diagnosed  with brain cancer started a group called  stupid cancer as a support group and I  learned that in this group it's not  pediatrics it's not older they have very  special problems so sexuality you going  on your first date what do you say when  do you say it you want fertility you go  into the job interview who do you say to  when they need support groups and it's  really great that now this is getting  the attention it deserves you feel like  you touched on some of them there but  did they sort of figure out some of the  reasons for the social issues so the  interesting they found that nearly 32  percent had persistently low level of  social functioning throughout the period  that they were studied and when they  looked at issues involved a lot of it  was people who were off treatment and so  I think the big issue here is that even  though you may have been cured of your  cancer there are survivorship issues  things where you're worried about  negative impacts on financial situations  on your relationship on whether you're  gonna be able to have kids your body  image on what your work plans are going  to be so I think one of the big  takeaways is that we need to have  support not just for people during the  time of their treatment but in terms of  reintegrating them back into their life  when they have become a survivor  were there other recommended strategies  for this jump in terms of dealing with  us yeah one of them is you know support  group well it should be in your age  group yeah you don't want to have  somebody who's 18 go into a support  group whether somebody who's 90 there's  a different set of issues and things  that you would expect intuitively  obvious like in the hospital have places  that are pleasant you know have for the  kids have games things that are like you  know things that they like to do not  things that somebody again who is 60 and  then again understanding as Tara was  saying that there are special issues so  you may survive the cancer and then  later on have even secondary medical  issues in the past they didn't survive  to get the secondary medical issues  problems with their heart and other  problems so it's great that this is  getting focus on it now and we need to  really specifically address these issues  all right finally a new take on wearable  technology one that can translate motion  into words new research in a journal  published by the public library of  science looked at a new glove that can  decode the American Sign Language  alphabet it then transmits the words to  electronic devices the glove was created  by researchers at the University of  California San Diego it costs less than  a hundred dollars to make and the study  authors believe the technology will have  multiple applications in the future one  word wow that is just wonderful and  thank you to Caitlin Luna from Gallaudet  University who taught me how to say  amazing the difference that just a bit  of technology can live forever  that's six four opens up the world and  connects us all doctors Jon LaPook and  Tara Narula thank you very much

